<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lu Xun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1881–1936</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Zhou Shuren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lu Xun, the pen name of Zhou Shuren, was a Chinese writer regarded as the founder of modern Chinese literature. His short stories in the vernacular, such as 'A Madman's Diary' (1918) and 'The True Story of Ah Q', attacked the traditions he blamed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s weakness and made him a leading voice of the New Culture Movement. In his last years he was a central figure of the League of Left-Wing Writers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lu-xun.jpg" alt="Lu Xun" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LuXun1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
